{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"# Context Graph\n\nA typed, evidence-backed graph of how everything in a repository connects —\ncode, docs, conversations, commits — derived live from history that already\nexists (git and [Conversation Ledger](https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger)),\nnot stored as a second system of record.\n\nMost of what a repository knows lives in the connections between its\nartifacts: a file imports another, a conversation touches a file, a commit\nmodifies it, a bridged conversation continues an earlier one. Those\nrelationships exist in the underlying history but are never addressable as\nstructure. Context Graph derives them into one typed graph, every edge\ntraceable to the evidence that justifies it.\n\nContext Graph is deliberately a **data structure, not a product surface**. It\ndoes not commit to any one consumer or export format. Expected downstream\nconsumers — each a separate concern, possibly a separate repo — include:\n\n- **agent traversal tooling** — an LLM agent walking the graph to help a human\n  with a task (humans are not expected to navigate it directly);\n- **training pipelines** — future experiments and productionized versions of\n  [tagseq2tagseq](https://github.com/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq)-like models\n  that train by traversing text-attributed graphs, including e.g. a\n  `torch.utils.data.Dataset` wrapper;\n- possibly **[intent-recall](https://github.com/evintunador/intent-recall)**,\n  whose relationship to this tool (parallel consumer of the ledger vs.\n  dependent on this graph) is undecided.\n\nThis repository is intentionally at the design stage. Nothing here is locked\nin — it records current impressions and the shape of the problem, not a\ncommitted API. See [product intent](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) and the\n[WIP technical design](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md).\n\n## Where it sits\n\n- **conversation-ledger** — neutral, immutable store of conversation events;\n  the first source Context Graph reads, alongside git history itself.\n- **turnbridge** — captures/moves conversations between CLIs; already writes\n  `continuation` lineage links into the ledger for this tool to surface.\n- **intent-recall** — early pre-requirements design stage; may end up a\n  sibling reader of the ledger or a consumer of this graph.\n- **context-graph** (this repo) — derives the explicit relationship graph over\n  git + ledger history.\n- **tagseq2tagseq** — a nearly finished MVP research project that trains\n  models by traversing a text-attributed document graph. It is *inspiration*\n  for what a training-side consumer looks like, not an integration target.\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE)\n=====WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN=====\n3-**Status:** exploratory impressions, nothing locked in — including the\n4-implementation language. This records how the problem looks after building\n5:conversation-ledger and turnbridge, so later work does not start cold.\n6-\n7-## Node model (draft)\n--\n34-  uncommitted state can identify that state but generally cannot reconstruct\n35-  it — it may reference content that no longer exists anywhere.\n36:  conversation-ledger is expected to record more granular worktree state,\n37-  which may close this gap; this design should track that work rather than\n38-  solve it here.\n--\n80-\n81-1. **Conversation Ledger**, via its library API (`readEvents`, `sortEvents`)\n82:   or `cledger export`, grouped by `conversation.id` — and read for *all*\n83-   users' records, since the graph is a whole-project history. (Practical\n84-   consequence: the graph over collaborators' conversations is only as\n--\n132-- **Consumer API.** What surface downstream tools consume: an in-process\n133-  library, a serialized snapshot on request, both?\n134:- **Language.** Genuinely indifferent. Reads TS (conversation-ledger) either\n135:  natively or via `cledger export`; feeds Python-side consumers eventually.\n136-  Whatever fits.\n137-- **Relationship to intent-recall.** Parallel le