{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Text Attributed Graph Sequence to Text Attributed Graph Sequence (TAGSeq2TAGSeq)\n2\t\n3\tA framework for training language models on **graph-structured text** — where documents are nodes and hyperlinks or import dependencies are edges. Rather than treating corpora as flat token streams, this project makes the link structure a first-class part of both training and inference.\n4\t\n5\tSee [INSTRUCTIONS.md](INSTRUCTIONS.md) for full pipeline, training, and generation commands.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## The Core Idea\n10\t\n11\tMost text corpora have implicit graph structure that standard language model training ignores. A Wikipedia article on *Fluid dynamics* links to *Hydraulics*, *Archimedes' screw*, and *Lever*. A Python file `mcts.py` imports `chess/board.py`, `chess/move.py`, and `model/tensorflow/model.py`. These links are not noise — they signal that the linked content is semantically relevant to the current document.\n12\t\n13\tTAGSeq2TAGSeq exploits this structure in two ways:\n14\t\n15\t1. **Graph traversal packs related documents together.** Instead of sampling random token windows, a graph traversal (BFS or DFS) walks the link graph and fills each training sequence with documents that are topologically close to one another.\n16\t\n17\t2. **A custom attention mask decides what each token can see.** At minimum, documents are causally isolated from one another — tokens in one article cannot attend to tokens in an unrelated article packed into the same sequence. Optionally, when document A explicitly links to document B and both are in the batch, tokens in A can attend back across the document boundary into B.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## Attention Masks\n22\t\n23\tThe attention pattern is the heart of the method. The four conditions below are\n24\t**all shown on the same 16k-token batch** (one repository from The Stack), ordered\n25\tby the amount of attention they permit (and thus FLOPs per token):\n26\t`doc_causal` < `cross_doc_link` ≤ `doc_concat_link` ≤ `doc_concatenated`. The two\n27\t`doc_concat_*` masks exist as **compute controls**: they grant *more* attention\n28\tthan `cross_doc_link` but with no inference-time linking, so a `cross_doc_link`\n29\twin can be attributed to the linking inductive bias rather than to extra compute.\n30\t\n31\t### `doc_causal` — document-isolated causal attention\n32\t\n33\tEach document attends only to itself. The mask is a block-diagonal of lower triangles: no information crosses document boundaries. This is the baseline — equivalent to training on independently sampled documents, but with the efficiency benefit of packing many short documents into one long sequence.\n34\t\n35\t![doc_causal mask on The Stack](docs/images/mask_doc_causal.png)\n36\t\n37\t*Each diagonal block is one Python file. Blue dashed lines mark document boundaries. Documents are completely isolated from one another.*\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t### `cross_doc_link` — link-aware cross-document attention\n42\t\n43\tWhen document A contains a link to document B (and B is present in the batch), tokens in A *from the link position onward* are granted read-access to all tokens in B. The causal structure within each document is preserved; the cross-document grants are asymmetric (A can read B, but B cannot read A unless it also links back).\n44\t\n45\t![cross_doc_link mask on The Stack](docs/images/mask_cross_doc_link.png)\n46\t\n47\t*Python files from one repository. Where a file imports an earlier file in the batch, its rows extend leftward as a black block — but only from the import line down, so each grant block's top edge sits below the source file's start.*\n48\t\n49\tThis teaches the model a grounded form of cross-document reasoning: when you encounter an import or a hyperlink, the full content of the referenced document is available in your attention context.\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t### `doc_concat_link` — whole-document link concatenation (compute control)\n54\t\n55\tIdentical to `cross_doc_link` except a detected link grants attention from the *entire* source document, not just from the link position onward. Equivalent to concatenating the source document onto its target. This strictly enlarges every `cross_doc_link` grant, so it isolates the effect of the precise link-position gating: same connectivity, more attention, no inference-time link prediction.\n56\t\n57\t![doc_concat_link mask on The Stack](docs/images/mask_doc_concat_link.png)\n58\t\n59\t*Same batch as `cross_doc_link` above. Each grant block now extends up to the top of its source file — compare the tall bottom-left block here against the shorter one in the previous figure, which started at the import line.*\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t### `doc_concatenated` — connected-component concatenation (compute control)\n64\t\n65\tDocuments that form a connected component in the link graph (e.g. files within a repository that import one another) are merged into a single causally-concatenated super-document: full causal attention across their combined tokens. Components that share no links remain isolated, so the mask is a block-diagonal of *larger* triangles — one per component, not one per document.\n66\t\n67\t![doc_concatenated mask on The Stack](docs/images/mask_doc_concatenated.png)\n68\t\n69\t*All files in this batch are import-connected, so they merge into a single super-document triangle — the per-file boundaries (blue dashed lines) are ignored entirely. Had the batch contained an unrelated, unlinked file, it would sit in its own separate triangle.*\n70\t\n71\tThis grants the most attention of the four conditions, with no inference-time linking — the upper compute control.\n72\t\n73\t---\n74\t\n75\t## Graph Traversal\n76\t\n77\tHow documents are ordered within a sequence matters for `cross_doc_link`: a document can only attend to documents that appear *before* it in the sequence. The traversal strategy controls this ordering.\n78\t\n79\t- **BFS** (breadth-first): explores the neighbourhood of a seed document level by level. Documents close in graph distance are packed together and predecessors tend to appear before successors. This is the preferred strategy for `cross_doc_link` training.\n80\t- **DFS** (depth-first): follows one path deep into the graph before backtracking. Produces longer chains of topically related documents.\n81\t- **Random walk**: a Markov walk with restart probability, producing soft locality without strict BFS ordering.\n82\t- **Random**: uniform random selection — equivalent to standard document sampling, used as a control baseline.\n83\t\n84\tWithin each strategy, a token budget and per-document token cap control sequence length and ensure no single document dominates the pack.\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## Datasets\n89\t\n90\tThe framework currently supports four pretokenized datasets and has several more in progress, spanning three distinct link-structure modalities.\n91\t\n92\t### Ready\n93\t\n94\t| Dataset | Edge type | Nodes | Tokens |\n95\t|---------|-----------|-------|--------|\n96\t| **SimpleWiki** | Markdown hyperlinks | 275k | ~108M |\n97\t| **EnWikiSource** | Markdown hyperlinks | 662k | ~612M |\n98\t| **The Stack** | Python `import` statements | 3.56M | ~8.7B |\n99\t| **arXiv (unarXive 2024)** | `\\cite{}` citations | 2.20M | ~10B |\n100\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_019kEKP5URj1aWVFPUHkKMAC","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"324e5de5bd856853a4c994915cf2b93c8a3d6e6b","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-611c41f9b084ce52c864f980e32909b1e02786cc916d9eeaa1d5f7ecedf3b5a2","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"session_id":"a044dbb5-5e38-4400-bb2a-97061ee33f87","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.241"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-24T03:25:23.923Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a0468aee6692a7819","cwd"