{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"43:import { captureRules, collectEnvValues, loadConfig, type CledgerConfig } from \"./redact/config.js\";\n44:import { addKnownSecrets, loadKnownSecrets } from \"./redact/known-secrets.js\";\n163:  const config = await loadConfig(repo.root);\n176:  // (rules.length > 0); known-secrets additionally requires its opt-in flag.\n179:    if (config.redact?.knownSecrets === true) {\n180:      const known = await loadKnownSecrets(repo);\n181:      if (known.length > 0) extraValues.push({ ruleId: \"known-secret\", values: known });\n208:    const known = new Set(existing.map((l) => parseEventLine(l).id));\n210:      if (known.has(e.id)) return false;\n211:      known.add(e.id);\n237:  const known = new Set(existing.map((l) => parseEventLine(l).id));\n238:  const fresh = events.filter((e) => !known.has(e.id));\n561: * — a read must never fail because detection did. The known gap the cursor\n568:    const config = await loadConfig(repo.root);\n853:      \"  cledger allow <fingerprint>   mark a fingerprint as a known false positive\\n\" +\n881:    const config = await loadConfig(repo.root);\n965:  const config = await loadConfig(repo.root);\n1058:   * known-secrets store (0 unless `redact.knownSecrets` is on and this was a\n1059:   * `--pattern` redaction that matched at least one not-already-known value).\n1061:  knownSecretsRemembered: number;\n--- filter\n  if (scope !== null) {\n    anchors = await resolveAnchors(repo, anchors, scope, readNote);\n  }\n  const events: EvidenceEvent[] = [];\n  for (const anchor of anchors) {\n    events.push(...(await readNote(anchor)));\n  }\n  events.push(...(await readPending(repo)));\n  const filtered = events.filter(\n    (e) =>\n      (!opts.kind || e.kind === opts.kind) &&\n      (!opts.source || e.producer.source === opts.source) &&\n      (!opts.model || e.producer.model === opts.model) &&\n      (!opts.conversation || e.conversation?.id === opts.conversation ||\n        e.conversation?.id.startsWith(opts.conversation)),\n  );\n  return sortEvents(dedupById(filtered));\n}\n\n/**\n * Collapse events that share an id but live under different anchor commits.\n *\n * Append-time dedup only ever consults the note of the commit being written\n * to (see `appendEvents`), so it cannot see a copy filed under a different\n * anchor. Any capture that re-reads transcript lines it already ingested\n * *after* HEAD moved therefore files a second copy of the same events against\n--- POLICY_SPLIT rest (headings + §4)\n8:## 0. Where the note came from\n17:## 1. Is it a three-way split or a two-way one?\n60:## 2. The tier is not a property of a field\n94:### The undisclosed-drop gap\n116:## 3. Which data entries the split has to cover\n167:## 4. Unified code system, or per-data-type principle?\n223:## 5. Concerns, and where this cuts against the project's aims\n279:## 6. What I'd change in the note itself\n## 4. Unified code system, or per-data-type principle?\n\n**Neither extreme. A thin shared spine, adapter-owned rendering, and a shared\nconformance suite.**\n\nAgainst full unification: the three targets' containers have almost nothing in\ncommon — a JSONL `parentUuid` chain, paired rollout lines in a session\ndirectory, and a payload shelled into `opencode import` against a shared\nSQLite DB. A generic tier-aware field-mapping engine over those three would be\nmostly escape hatches, and the escape hatches are where all the actual\nbehavior lives. Building it would add a layer without removing a decision.\n\nAgainst pure principle: prose discipline already failed once, visibly. The\nCodex model-id drop is the proof — every adapter is trusted to remember a rule\nwritten in a design doc, and one of them didn't.\n\nThe seam is that **tier 1 and the *accounting* of tier 2 are unifiable; the\n*realization* of tier 2 and all of tier 3 are not.** Concretely, three pieces:\n\n1. **A shared portable-turn normalizer.** One function from `summary.events`\n   to an ordered `PortableTurn[