{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"CONSTRAINTS.md\nDEFERRED_IDEAS.md\nDESIGN_DECISIONS.md\nEVALUATION.md\nEVIDENCE.md\nPRODUCT_INTENT.md\nWIP_INFORMATION_DYNAMICS.md\nWIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md\nThu Jul 30 07:50:37 2026 -0700\ndocs/DEFERRED_IDEAS.md:4:defined. Recorded so they are not lost, not because they are planned.\ndocs/DEFERRED_IDEAS.md:18:or only in an unindexed historical record, into the model's context — is bounded\ndocs/DEFERRED_IDEAS.md:41:Ledger.\ndocs/DEFERRED_IDEAS.md:52:preference for keeping outcomes recorded rather than discarded after a check\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:6:exists to prevent, so each records the failure it guards against.\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:15:reasoning, it validates that reasoning instead of the record, the two channels\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:18:## C2. The checker must have no path to the ledger\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:20:Deny shell access. Conversation Ledger stores evidence in git notes rather than\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:22:raw record — this is an absence of capability, not a policy.\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:24:Note the hole: the intent tree is committed to the working tree (D10), so a\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:25:filesystem-capable checker *can* read stored intent records. This is acceptable —\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:53:## C5. No model-authored prose in stored records\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:55:Stored records contain pointers into the ledger, verbatim spans, content hashes,\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:60:error the project exists to remove, and any stored prose can be read back as\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:79:Note the distinction this creates. \"The quote is real and unaltered\" is a\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:84:## C6. Never write derived data into Conversation Ledger\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:86:Intent Recall maintains its own append-only log on a separate ref.\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:93:Derived records are a pure function of (ledger events, extractor version, human\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:94:corrections). Deleting and rebuilding must be lossless. Every record carries the\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:101:## C8. Corrections key to evidence, never to derived records\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:103:A human correction points at `(event_id, span)` in the immutable ledger, never at\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:104:a derived record id, and is stored outside the derived tree so regeneration\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:118:`--full-refresh` overwrites manual corrections and that preserving them\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:137:(Certificate Transparency, Sigstore). Those defend against a dishonest log\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:142:## C9. Depend on Conversation Ledger's query interface, never its storage layout\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:144:No direct reads of `refs/notes/*`, no assumptions about worktree or branch\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:147:*Guards against:* breakage from ledger-internal changes. Storage may become\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:178:grade its own test. Note that the opposite choice is correct for\ndocs/CONSTRAINTS.md:196:being judged: ledger events filtered by timestamp, indexes rebuilt under that\nREADME.md:3:Finding out what a human actually meant, from the record of what they actually\n\n--- context-graph nodes/edges\nsurvives `git mv`. Two known complications, recorded rather than solved:\n\n- The ledger currently captures a *fingerprint* of uncommitted state (sha256\n  of `git status --porcelain`), not its content, so a node keyed on\n  uncommitted state can identify that state but generally cannot reconstruct\n  it — it may reference content that no longer exists anywhere.\n  conversation-ledger is expected to record more granular worktree state,\n  which may close this gap; this design should track that work rather than\n  solve it here.\n- Rename detection (git's `--follow` machinery) is heuristic, which is in\n  tension with deterministic rebuilds; the derivation must pin whatever\n  detection parameters it uses.\n\n## Edge model (draft)\n\nEdges are typed and evidence-bearing: `{src, dst, kind, basis}` where `basis`\npoints at the events / lines / commits that justify the edge. There is **no\nconfidence field**: every edge is mechanically derived from explicit\nevidence, so kind + basis carries all the trust information there is.\nAnything requiring semantic judgment (a conversation that \"discusses the auth\nmodule\" without ever naming a path, embedding similarity, model-inferred\nlinks) is out of scope here — that class of derivation belongs downstream,\nplausibly in intent-recall.\n\nCandidate kinds, all mechanical:\n\n- `continuation` — conversation→conversation, from turnbridge's ledger\n  `links` (`rel:\"continues\"`). Bridged conversations carry\n  `imported_through_seq`; per turnbridge's design, target turns up through it\n  are known copies of the source, so consumers dedup across this edge (the\n  copied prefix is reachable through the pointer, not re-materialized).\n- `anchored_to` — event→commit, from ledger `context.head`: the code state a\n  turn actually ran against (per event, since context is recorded per event).\n- `modifies` — commit→file, from git history itself.\n- `imports` — file→file, from import/require statements.\n- `references` — doc→file (and doc→doc), from links in documentation.\n- `touches` — event→file, from file paths appearing literally in tool-call\n  content blocks (`Read`/`Edit`/`Bash`) and message text. Literal paths only;\n  no semantic matching.\n- `re_anchored_to` — commit→commit, surfacing the ledger's `re_anchor`\n  mapping events (squash/rebase supersession) as structure rather than\n  resolving them away invisibly.\n\nExplicitly dropped from earlier drafts: co-occurrence edges (`shared_file`,\n`same_author`, `temporal_adjacency` — projections and priors a consumer can\ncompute, not evidence), conversation-level `supersession` (nothing in the\necosystem emits one), `decision` nodes (no producer exists; intent-recall is\ntoo early to design against), and per-edge confidence scores.\n\n## Inputs\n\n1. **Conversation Ledger**, via its library API (`readEvents`, `sortEvents`)\n   or `cledger export`, grouped b