{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"The survey came back: **context-graph and intent-recall are design-docs only — no `src/`, no tests, no code.** The only real cledger integration outside cledger itself is turnbridge. That reframes the whole thing, so here's what I found and what I need from you.\n\n## What the docs actually propose (in one breath)\n\nThe turnbridge branch (`design/policy-split`) is three docs. Two are turnbridge-internal (how fabrication degrades data across CLIs, how multi-hop bridging compounds that damage). The third, `TODO_HANDOFF.md`, spins out one item to cledger: turnbridge writes a custom `continuation` event into the ledger, other tools are supposedly starting to parse it, so cledger should \"govern\" third-party event kinds. The cledger branch's `docs/EXTENSION_GOVERNANCE.md` settles that as:\n\n1. Don't put the writing tool into event ids (no change).\n2. Identify third-party kinds by `(producer.tool, kind)` — no renaming, no migration (no change).\n3. Migration pattern noted for later (no change).\n4. **New:** a persisted registry in `.cledger.json` — `{tool, kind, version, schema}` — so consumers can discover which third-party vocabularies a ledger contains, with shape validation on write for registered kinds.\n5. Document that `appendEvents` installs a pre-push hook.\n\nPlus three new `readEvents` filters: `tool`, `kind` as an array, `linkRel`.\n\n## What checks out, and what doesn't\n\nVerified against code: the identity-subset claims are right (`src/schema.ts:252-265`), `readEvents` filters in memory so filters are pure ergonomics (`src/store.ts:318`), and turnbridge really does have the fetch-everything workaround (`turnbridge/src/conversations.ts:36-40`).\n\nThree things the docs get wrong or thin:\n\n- **The motivating premise is a design doc, not code.** \"context-graph parses turnbridge's payload\" — context-graph has zero code (last commit Aug 4). intent-recall same (Jul 30). Today there is exactly one producer and one consumer of `continuation`, and they're the same file (`turnbridge/src/lineage.ts`). The \"public interface with no owner\" problem is hypothetical.\n- **Decision 5 says \"don't add an opt-out\" — one already exists.** `.cledger.json` → `transport.hook: false` (`src/transport.ts:172`). So Decision 5 is just \"write a paragraph.\"\n- **Decision 4 is the only real build, and it's the least argued.** It's a schema registry — cledger would validate JSON shapes for other tools' events. The doc rejects the cheaper alternative (turnbridge exports `readLineage`, context-graph imports it) in one line, even though the *turnbridge* lineage doc explicitly proposed that as option one. It also doesn't say what \"schema\" means (JSON Schema? a TS type? which validator dependency?), or what happens when the persisted registry in a committed `.cledger.json` disagrees with the version of turnbridge that's installed.\n\n## Questions\n\n**1. Is the actual problem you're feeling one of these, or something else?**\n   - (a) You want context-graph/intent-recall to exist and be able to walk conversation lineage without importing turnbridge.\n   - (b) You're annoyed that turnbridge has to fetch every event and filter client-side.\n   - (c) You want cledger's library surface to be a *designed* front door rather than the retrofitted one `src/index.ts` admits to being.\n   - (d) Something about the multi-hop degradation problem in turnbridge, which is the meatier design question in those docs and is entirely turnbridge-side.\n\n   These point at very different amounts of work. (b) is a 30-line change. (a) needs the registry or a turnbridge library export. (c) is a real project. (d) isn't a cledger feature at all.\n\n**2. Are you okay building for consumers that don't exist yet?** The registry (Decision 4) is speculative until context-graph has code. The three filters are cheap and one has a real user today (turnbridge's kind-array case). My inclination: ship the filters + docs now, hold the registry until there's a second real consumer. Do you want me to hold to that, or do you want the registry built because you're about to start context-graph?\n\n**3. Should cledger validate other tools' payloads at all?** That's the core philosophical shift in Decision 4. Today cledger's contract is \"never interpret content.\" A registry with schema validation means a turnbridge upgrade that changes its payload could get its writes *rejected* by a stale `.cledger.json` in someone's repo. If you'd rather the rule stay \"whoever emits a vocabulary publishes its reader\" (turnbridge exports `readLineage`; context-graph imports turnbridge), that's zero cledger work and the turnbridge doc already endorsed it.\n\n**4. If a registry does happen, where should it live?** Committed `.cledger.json` (travels with clones, but versioning conflicts as above), or the tool registers at runtime on first append (process-local, what the doc rejected)? The doc's stated reason for persistence is discovery — but a consumer could just as well discover vocabularies by reading distinct `producer.tool` values from the events themselves, with no registry.\n\n**5. Does \"the handoff is settled\" match your understanding?** Both docs say decisions 1–5 are settled and the build is ready to hand off. You said you haven't read them. I'd treat nothing as settled until you say so — is that right, or did you sign off on any of it in an earlier session?\n\n**6. Scope check on the turnbridge side.** The turnbridge docs' sections B (\"stays with us\") contain real bugs — turnbridge ignores `producer.model`/`provider` that cledger has shipped since 0.12.0 and still guesses the provider by string-splitting the model id; and the \"copy from the original, never from the copy\" fix for compounding degradation. Neither needs cledger changes. Do you want those on the table, or is this session strictly cledger?\n\nMy recommendation if you want a default: do the `readEvents` filters and the docs (items 1, 3, 4, 5 of the handoff), skip the registry, and add a note to the turnbridge TODO that `readLineage` is the published reader for `continuation`. That's small, un-speculative, and closes the only papercut with a real user today.\n\nresult: Reviewed both design branches; the cledger handoff is mostly no-ops plus three cheap read filters, and its one real build (a schema registry) is motivated by consumers (context-graph, intent-recall) that have no code yet — six clarifying questions above, recommended default is filters + docs, no registry.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"324e5de5bd856853a4c994915cf2b93c8a3d6e6b","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-50560ebd916342463331e911affb33f626189d4749543af6f745fdde81e16441","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-fable-5","session_id":"a044dbb5-5e38-4400-bb2a-97061ee33f87","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.241"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-24T02:24:45.162Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"The survey came back: **context-graph and intent-recall are design-docs only — no `src/`, no tests, no code.** The only real cledger integration outside cledger itself is turnbridge. That reframes the whole thing, so here's what I found and what I need from you.\n\n## What the docs actually propose (in one breath)\n\nThe turnbridge branch (`design/policy-split`) is three docs. Two are turnbridge-internal (how fabrication degrades data across CLIs, how multi-hop bridging compounds that damage). The third, `TODO_HANDOFF.md`, spins out one item to cledger: turnbridge writes a custom `continuation` event into the ledger, other tools are supposedly starting to parse it