{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"The tagseq2tagseq survey is in, and together with intent-recall's docs it settles the demand question. Here's the full picture and my answers.\n\n## 1. The demand is real, and it's more diverse than \"conversations plus edges\"\n\nFour customers, each wanting something slightly different from the same substrate:\n\n| Customer | What it writes | What it needs that cledger's store doesn't have today |\n|---|---|---|\n| **cledger** (conversations) | turns, reasoning, session state, file snapshots | — (it *is* the store today) |\n| **turnbridge** | `continuation` edges between conversations | to be a peer producer, not a guest |\n| **intent-recall** | corrections log; records are \"pointer into the ledger (event id + span) + content hash + typed edges\" (`docs/DESIGN_DECISIONS.md` D11, D13, D15) | **its own ref**, separate from evidence, so derived data can be wiped and rebuilt (D13: \"must be architecturally impossible\" to mix them) |\n| **tagseq2tagseq / tunalab** | run records: git commit + content-addressed patch for uncommitted diffs, resolved config, eval metrics, `resume-from` chains linking runs; plus `provenance/ledger.yaml` mapping paper claim-keys → run fields with `derived {op, operands}` edges | today these are JSON files in the working tree (19MB, mostly gitignored) written by `tunalab.reproducibility`; nothing links them to commits the way cledger anchors do |\n\nSo it's not one extra customer — it's a pattern: **append-only records, anchored to a commit, with typed links to other records, synced through git**. That's a package, and I'd now say build it as one rather than a module boundary. The thing that tipped me is intent-recall's separate-ref requirement: cledger hard-codes a single `refs/notes/cledger`, and \"one namespace per producer, independently wipeable and pushable\" is a store feature, not a conversation feature.\n\nOne caution from the tunalab case: git notes are fine for run *records* and patches; they are not fine for checkpoints. The store needs a stated size policy (records carry pointers/hashes to large artifacts, never the artifacts) — same \"coordinates, never content\" rule cledger already learned from the scan-report loop.\n\n## 2. The question I asked badly, in plain terms\n\nEvery cledger event today carries `conversation: {id, seq}` — \"which conversation is this in, and what position.\" The store uses it for two things: sorting events into order, and as part of the hash that makes the event's id. turnbridge also uses it, to attach its `continuation` edge to the target conversation so the edge travels with it.\n\nThe question was: should the *generic* store have a built-in idea of \"records belong to a group and have an order inside that group,\" or should it be flat records + links only, with grouping left to each producer?\n\nLooking at the four customers: conversations need it (turns in order), turnbridge needs it (edge rides with a conversation), tunalab runs need it (config/patch/metrics belong to a run), intent-recall's corrections log needs order. **My answer: keep it, as an optional generic field with a neutral name** (`stream: {id, seq}` or `group`). cledger's `conversation` becomes \"what stream means for conversations.\" Nothing is forced to use it.\n\n## 3. Migration: wiping is fine, and it unlocks a cleaner envelope\n\nSince you're the only user and are willing to drop history, the constraint that shaped Decisions 1–2 (never change event ids) is gone. That lets the envelope be designed right instead of compatibly:\n\n```\nid            ev2-<hash of identity fields>\nkind          open string; owned by (producer.tool, kind)\noccurred_at   / recorded_at\nproducer      { tool, version, ... }        ← the generic attribution\nstream?       { id, seq }                   ← optional grouping + order\nlinks?        [{ rel, target }]             ← typed edges, rel owned by producer\ncontent       JSON, never interpreted by the store\ncontext       { head, branch, ... }         ← the anchor; store-owned\nraw?          producer's source material\n```\n\n`actor` (human/agent/system, git email vs model id) leaves the envelope and becomes cledger vocabulary inside `content`. Redaction, secret scan, dedup, reachability scoping, transport, re-anchor all stay in the store, unchanged in substance.\n\nPractical note on wiping: the notes are pushed in nine repos, so it's `git push origin :refs/notes/cledger` per remote plus local ref deletion — a small script, and I'd write it to be run by you rather than run it myself. A rewrite-in-place tool is probably also only ~100 lines given the `renormalize` precedent, so keep that option open if you'd rather not lose the 130-odd anchors.\n\n## 4. Names\n\nChecked npm availability. The ecosystem convention is two-word descriptive hyphenated names (`conversation-ledger`, `context-graph`, `intent-recall`), so I'd match that:\n\n- **`git-ledger`** (free) — my pick. Plain, says exactly what it is, and makes `conversation-ledger` read as \"a ledger of conversations built on git-ledger.\" CLI could be `gledger` or `ledger`.\n- **`git-annals`** (free) — annals = chronological append-only record; a bit literary but precise.\n- **`git-substrate`** (free) — intent-recall's own docs already call the ledger \"the source substrate,\" so the word is in use in your ecosystem.\n- **`record-ledger`** (free) — generic but bland.\n- Taken on npm, in case you liked them: `sediment`, `strata`, `substrate`, `refstore`, `gitledger`.\n\n## Where this leaves the two design branches\n\n- cledger's `EXTENSION_GOVERNANCE.md`: Decisions 1–2 were correct *under the no-migration constraint*, which you've now lifted; Decision 4 (registry) dissolves into \"store validates the envelope, each producer validates and publishes a reader for its own kinds\"; Decision 5 is already implemented (`transport.hook: false`). The three read filters (`tool`, `kind[]`, `linkRel`) survive as store-layer filters. I'd retire the doc rather than build from it.\n- turnbridge's handoff section A needs a one-paragraph update saying the target moved.\n\nWhat I'd do next, if you want to proceed: write a short design doc for the store package (envelope, multi-ref namespaces, size policy, what moves out of cledger), then extract. I have not written anything yet — this session has been read-only.\n\nresult: Confirmed a separate generic store package is justified — four real customers (cledger, turnbridge, intent-recall's separate-ref corrections log, tunalab run provenance) share \"append-only records anchored to commits with typed links\"; proposed an `ev2` envelope with optional `stream:{id,seq}` and `actor` moved out, recommended the name `git-ledger` (free on npm), and concluded the existing governance doc should be retired rather than built.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"324e5de5bd856853a4c994915cf2b93c8a3d6e6b","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-5183d0b3af4052aad2c0dc93f07af76a8b8e8f72cc4e8b377c1aa1cf257d32f9","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-fable-5","session_id":"a044dbb5-5e38-4400-bb2a-97061ee33f87","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.241"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-24T03:27:54.935Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"The tagseq2tagseq survey is in, and together with intent-recall's docs it settles the demand question. Here's the full picture and my answers.\n\n## 1. The demand is real, and it's more diverse than \"conversations plus edges\"\n\nFour customers, each wanting something slightly different from the same substrate:\n\n| Customer | What it writes | What it needs that cledger's store